    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "As a way to respond to 50 years of armed conflict, young Colombians are being framed as important peacebuilders on whom the future of the country—and of peace—depends. Yet, there has been significantly less effort invested in understanding the responses of young people to such framing. Focusing particularly on urban elite settings, my research explores how young Colombians in these contexts respond to narratives of citizenship after the armed conflict. Through semi-structured interviews, I construct a case study of 20 students in their junior and senior years in private high schools in Cali, Colombia, and argue that they expand, challenge, and appropriate such narratives in ways that directly respond to their historical, social, economic, and personal contexts. I explore the implications of such findings for citizenship education—in Colombia and around the world.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Lundy Mogollón, G. (2024). \"Build The New Nation That Is In Your Hands\": Young Colombians' Narratives of Citizenship After Armed Conflict . Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/hf089xk3171. https://doi.org/10.25740/hf089xk3171.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
